# Contributing

Refer to the [Ansible community guide](https://docs.ansible.com/ansible/devel/community/index.html).

## Development environment

The local development and test toolchain (ansible, ansible-lint, yamllint, molecule, pytest, …) is managed with [uv](https://docs.astral.sh/uv/).
The dependencies and their pinned versions live in `pyproject.toml` and `uv.lock`.
This is a dev-only environment and is never published as part of the collection (the collection itself is defined by `galaxy.yml`).

Set up the environment and run the tooling:

```bash
# Create/refresh the virtualenv (.venv) from the lockfile
uv sync

# Run any tool inside the environment
uv run ansible-lint
uv run yamllint .
uv run pytest
uv run molecule test -s firstboot
```

Add or bump a dev dependency with `uv add --dev <pkg>`

## Opening a pull request

Keep the scope of a pull request as small as possible and give it a
[conventional commit](https://www.conventionalcommits.org/) title: the `PR labels` check
labels the pull request from the title (`feat:` → enhancement, `fix:` → bug,
`<type>!:` → major, `chore:`/`ci:`/`docs:` → skip-changelog) and fails unless the title or
a label matches a release-drafter category.
The label and the title become the entry in the generated changelog, so write the title
for the changelog reader. Fragments in `changelogs/fragments/` are only for notes a title
cannot carry, such as a `release_summary` or porting instructions under
`breaking_changes`; they are added on top of the title entry.

## Create a new release

1. Manually trigger the `Draft release` workflow. It resolves the version from the merged
   pull requests' labels, generates the changelog with antsibull-changelog and antsichaut,
   bumps `galaxy.yml` and opens a "Changelog updated" pull request.
2. Review and merge that pull request.
3. Publish the draft in the release section. The `release.yml` workflow builds the
   collection and publishes it to Ansible Galaxy.
